==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-proper-name.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[word]] or [[phrase]] that has [[noun]] part of speech and [[name]]s a specific object, usually capitalized, examples being [[Martin]] or [[New York]].
#: {{syn|en|proper noun<qq:sometimes [[Appendix:Glossary#strictly|strictly]] coordinate>|name|selfname}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1950|author=Bertrand Russell|title=The Principles of Mathematics
|passage=A '''proper name''', when it occurs in a proposition, is always, at least according to one of the possible ways of analysis (where there are several), the subject that the proposition or some subordinate constituent proposition is about, and not what is said about the subject.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1970|title=Speech acts|author=John R. Searle
|passage=We might clarify some of the points made in this chapter by comparing paradigm '''proper names''' with degenerate '''proper names''' like "the Bank of England".
|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=t3_WhfknvF0C&lpg=PA172&dq=%22obviously%20to%20refer%20to%20individuals%22&pg=PA173}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:W. H. Auden|year=1970|title=A Certain World|page=267|location=New York|isbn=0670209945|publisher=Viking Press|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/certainworldcomm00aude/page/267/mode/1up|passage='''Proper names''' are poetry in the raw. Like all poetry they are untranslatable. Someone who is translating into English a German novel, the hero of which is named Heinrich, will leave the name as it is; he will not Anglicize it into Henry.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|chapter=Names|title=Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy|author=Sam Cumming|passage=For instance, the '''proper name''' ‘Jessica Alba’ consists of two proper nouns: ‘Jessica’ and ‘Alba’.
|url=http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/names/}}
#* {{seeCites|en}}

====Usage notes====
* The term is usually synonymous with {{m|en|proper noun}} since dictionaries tend to define {{m|en|proper noun}} as including multi-word phrases. Some uses of {{m|en|proper noun}} are more restricted.

